	while (p && strlen(buf) > strlen(root)) {
		*p = '\0';
		count_assert(rmdir(buf) == 0);
		p = strrchr(buf, '/');
	}
```

Wait, let me trace: buf="/tmp/treefiles-test-XXX/nbd/TREE1023/TREE1023/FILE1023" (already with FILE stripped? No — buf contains FILE1023, and that's a file that has already been unlinked; the first strrchr gives the slash of /FILE1023 → *p=0 → buf=".../TREE1023/TREE1023" — strlen > strlen(root) ✓ rmdir ✓; next ".../nbd/TREE1023" ✓; ".../nbd" — strlen = strlen(root)+4 > strlen(root) ✓ rmdir; next buf=root → strlen == strlen(root) → loop ends. Then rmdir(root) ✓.

But note, the first iteration strips "FILE1023" off buf — good, since that's the file that was just deleted.

Also `char name[strlen(root) + sizeof("/nbd")];` — sizeof("/nbd") = 5 (including NUL), strlen(root)+5 — sufficient for root+"/nbd"+NUL. ✓

